.Dd August 16, 2026
.Dt BITCHAT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bitchat
.Nd terminal client for the BitChat Bluetooth mesh
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl i Ar index
.Op Fl n Ar nickname
.Op Fl v
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl n Ar nickname
.Fl L Ar port
.Op Fl C Ar host : Ns Ar port ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
joins the BitChat Bluetooth Low Energy mesh and shows public and private chat
in a terminal window.
Nearby devices form the mesh without infrastructure or accounts.
Public messages are signed and flooded with a hop limit; private messages run
inside a Noise
.Sy XX
session, so relays carry only ciphertext.
.Pp
The radio is driven through
.Xr bluetoothd 8 :
.Nm
registers a GATT application and an advertisement with BlueZ over D-Bus, and
never takes the adapter for itself.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d
Show transport chatter in the message pane: link state and connection
failures.
.Cm /debug
toggles it at run time.
.It Fl i Ar index
Use adapter
.Pa /org/bluez/hci Ns Ar index .
The default is 0.
.It Fl n Ar nickname
Announce this nickname.
The default comes from
.Ev USER .
.It Fl L Ar port
Run the mesh over TCP instead of Bluetooth, listening on
.Ar port .
Pass 0 for any free port.
.It Fl C Ar host : Ns Ar port
Dial another node.
Repeatable, and implies
.Fl L .
Nodes wired this way run the same mesh, with presence, relaying and Noise
sessions, and no radio involved.
It is how multi-hop behaviour is tested without three phones.
.It Fl v
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
Text typed at the prompt is sent to the public channel.
Lines that start with
.Sq /
are commands:
.Bl -tag -width "/msg peer text"
.It Cm /who
List reachable peers.
.It Cm /msg Ar peer Ar text
Send a private message.
.Ar peer
matches a nickname or peer ID prefix.
The first message opens a Noise session and is delivered when it completes.
.It Cm /nick Ar name
Change the announced nickname.
.It Cm /debug
Toggle the transport log.
.It Cm /id
Print your own peer ID.
.It Cm /quit
Leave the mesh and exit.
.El
.Sh TEXT
Message text is UTF-8.
Emoji shortcodes expand as you type: the name between two colons, such as
.Sq bone ,
becomes the glyph when the closing colon arrives.
Tab completes: a command at the start of the line, a peer name after
.Sq @
or after
.Cm /msg ,
and an emoji shortcode anywhere else.
One match is applied; several extend the input as far as they agree and are
listed.
A message that names you is shown in red and rings the bell.
Text that is not valid UTF-8 is dropped on receive, and control bytes never
reach the terminal.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/bitchat/identity
The Curve25519 and Ed25519 private keys, mode 0600.
The peer ID is the first eight bytes of the SHA-256 digest of the Curve25519
public key, so deleting this file gives the device a new identity.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bluetoothctl 1 ,
.Xr bluetoothd 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Nick Owens Aq Mt mischief@offblast.org
.Sh CAVEATS
Bluetooth is the only transport.
There is no Nostr path, no store-and-forward courier, and no file or voice
transfer.
Announcements carry the nickname and public keys in cleartext, so anyone in
radio range can enumerate participants.
